.The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (dst) {
    /* reassign page to dst after offset */
    get_page(page);
    sg_set_page(dst + j, page, plen, sg[i].offset);
    j++;
}

Reason:
The original condition only checks if `dst` is non-NULL, but does not verify that `plen` (the length of the current scatterlist segment to be transferred) is non-zero. When `plen` is zero (e.g., because `used` has been exhausted but the loop continues), this branch still executes, causing an extra page reassignment and thus a page reassignment overflow (one more page than necessary).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (dst && plen) {
    /* reassign page to dst */
    get_page(page);
    sg_set_page(dst + j, page, plen, sg[i].offset);
    j++;
}

Reason:
Adding `plen` to the condition ensures that the page reassignment only occurs when there is actual data length to transfer (`plen > 0`). This prevents the overflow by skipping zero-length segments, while still allowing legitimate transfers when `plen` is positive. The comment is also updated to reflect the removal of the obsolete offset argument.

